      "rev_n_1_73b728_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_73b728_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_73b728",
        "thought_text": "Thought-1: Calculate the earliest possible arrival at each station, considering all possible train combinations. This approach ensures we find the optimal path that allows for the maximum delay compensation.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_73b728_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_20b4277325",
        "created_at": 1794533304.056263
      },
      "rev_n_2_f18c82_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_f18c82_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_f18c82",
        "thought_text": "The current approach using Dijkstra's algorithm is correct, but it needs a slight modification to ensure we find the earliest time that allows us to reach station N in strictly less than 30 minutes. We should adjust our condition for checking the arrival time at station N to be exactly 30 minutes less than the planned arrival time.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_f18c82_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_ef5e369b4f",
        "created_at": 1794533336.1918178
      },
      "rev_n_3_c82ff0_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_c82ff0_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_c82ff0",
        "thought_text": "The error in the code is in the condition for checking if a train can be taken. It should be `new_arrival_time <= T + 1800` instead of `new_arrival_time < T + 1800`. This change allows trains that arrive exactly at the 1800-second threshold to be considered, which is crucial for finding the earliest possible booking time.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_c82ff0_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_629ab4c855",
        "created_at": 1794533369.7743998
      },
      "rev_n_4_583cb1_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_583cb1_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_583cb1",
